  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Salient Crgt obligated $868M in federal contracts in FY2026 to date. Salient Crgt won 57 awards so far, with an average award size of $15.2M, and obligations are up 6.3% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose from $240M in FY21 to $868M in FY26, with intermediate totals of $405M in FY22, $505M in FY23, $557M in FY24, and $816M in FY25. The General Services Administration is Salient Crgt's largest buyer with $584M in obligations in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $138M and the Department of Homeland Security at $54.4M. Computer Systems Design Services is the dominant market for Salient Crgt with $774M in obligations in FY2026 to date, well ahead of Engineering Services at $63.5M. Smaller markets include Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $17.8M and Other Computer Related Services at $9.9M. The fiscal-year series and top-agency and top-market breakdowns show a concentrated footprint in civilian IT work, anchored by a large GSA relationship and heavy exposure to Computer Systems Design Services.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Salient Crgt's biggest federal customers?",
        "body": "General Services Administration is Salient Crgt's largest buyer with $584M in obligations in FY2026 to date. Department of Veterans Affairs follows with $138M and Department of Homeland Security follows with $54.4M. Department of Defense has $49.2M and smaller buyers include Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation at $16.8M and Commodity Futures Trading Commission at $13.6M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and services drive Salient Crgt's obligations?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services drives Salient Crgt's obligations with $774M in FY2026 to date. Engineering Services contributes $63.5M and Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services contributes $17.8M. Other Computer Related Services and Custom Computer Programming Services add $9.9M and $1.7M respectively, with several smaller categories at or near zero."
      }
    ]
  },
